  "paras": [
   "USDA&rsquo;s 2027 county base values, the same on every grid in the county. Protection per acre is the value times your coverage level times a productivity factor of 60 to 150: grazing at 90% and 100 is $72.18 an acre. USDA sets irrigated hay lower here.",
   "At 90% coverage, across the 9 grids that touch the county: first the middle of the 9 for that pair of months (it can be a different grid in each row), then the lowest and highest. The premium is the full rate per $100 of protection; USDA pays 59% at 70%, 59% at 75%, 55% at 80%, 55% at 85%, 51% at 90%. Normal is the grid&rsquo;s average precipitation for those months. A year counts, of the 75 from 1951 to 2025, when the grid&rsquo;s index for those months came in under 90% of normal, which is when a 90% policy pays.",
   "The wettest pair of months here is May to June (9.50 in on the middle of the 9 grids) and the driest is January to February (1.92 in). Coverage costs least in April to May and most in November to December. The months to insure are the ones when a dry spell costs you grass or hay, not the cheapest ones.",
   "The index for each pair of months as a percent of normal (100 is a normal year), on the middle of the 9 grids (each grid has its own; the calculator shows yours). Shaded cells came in under 90%, where a 90% policy on those months would have paid. 2026 shows the 6 pairs of months USDA has published so far.",
   "Each grid&rsquo;s normal precipitation over a year, its driest year since 1951 as a percent of normal, 2025 as a percent of normal, and how many of 75 years as a whole came in under 90%. The driest year on the record here was 1988."
  ]
